[interviewer · RECOMMENDATION · 1:14] Okay. You assumed volume holds when prices go up 15% and that costs stay flat in dollars. If that fails, what is the biggest risk to this recommendation, and how would it change what you do?

[candidate · 1:20] Biggest risk is that volume drops enough to offset the price gain. At 15% higher prices, if traffic falls more than roughly 13%, revenue is flat or worse — and COGS in dollars falls with volume, but labor and rent largely don't, so we'd lose operating leverage. Fixed costs are about 14% overhead plus a chunk of the 22% labor, so a traffic decline hits margin harder than the model suggests.

The other risk is costs keep climbing — a 38% shock in two years means the trend may not be over, so a one-time 15% could be eaten within a year.

That changes the approach: phase it, 7–8% now and reassess, pilot in two or three regions against control stores, and pair it with hedging or longer-term bean contracts so we're not re-pricing every six months. I'd also lead with price increases on add-on items where attach is already rising, since those look least elastic.
